# Consumed by Workers Static Assets; never served as a file itself.
#
# These are the headers the Node server applied in code. They have to be
# restated here because static assets are served at the edge without the Worker
# script running.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'

# The shell and the service worker must never be served stale, or a deploy
# would not reach devices that already installed the app.
#
# That has to cover the scripts too, not just the HTML. Filenames carry no
# content hash, so a browser holding app.js from an hour ago will happily pair
# it with a freshly fetched index.html. Half-updated is worse than not updated:
# an old app.js reading a document the new one wrote sees no entries it
# recognises and renders an empty collection.
#
# `no-cache` means revalidate, not "don't store" — the ETag still turns almost
# every one of these into a 304, and the service worker still serves them
# offline.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/app.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/styles.css
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/lib/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
